<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Deira</h1><p class="st-meta">559 CE – 654 CE (approx.) · capital York</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Anglian kingdom of the Yorkshire Wolds and York, whose boys in a Roman slave market are said to have inspired Pope Gregory's mission to the English.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Deira developed from Anglian settlement in east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a>, perhaps taking over a British territory whose name it kept. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aelle-of-deira">Aelle</a> as its first king from 560. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> tells how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Gregory the Great</a>, seeing Deiran slave boys in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, punned that they were not <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a> but angels.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Deira's centre lay around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire-wolds">Wolds</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edwin-of-northumbria">Edwin</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aelle-of-deira">Aelle</a>, ruled all <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Northumbria</a> from 616, accepted baptism from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paulinus-of-york">Paulinus</a> at York in 627 and built the first church on the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york-minster">York Minster</a>. After his death in 633 Deira had its own kings again, including Oswine, killed on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiu">Oswiu</a> in 651.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Bernician kings repeatedly dominated Deira. Its sub-king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oethelwald-of-deira">Oethelwald</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswald-of-northumbria">Oswald</a>, supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penda-of-mercia">Penda</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiu">Oswiu</a> and disappeared after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-winwaed">Winwaed</a> in 655; Oswiu's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhfrith">Alhfrith</a> then ruled Deira under his father. Deira became the southern half of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria</a>, its name surviving in later sources.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-deira.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Deira" width="320" height="333"><figcaption>Image: myself · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Northumbria.rise.600.700.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>559 CE – 654 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>York</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Aelle, Edwin, Oswine</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglo-Saxon paganism; Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>United Kingdom</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Deira, Deifr</dd></dl></aside></div>
